{
 "id": "2022-adam-billboards",
 "name": "Billboards and Street Pleasantness in Kuala Lumpur (SP-Scheme)",
 "description": "Method replication: same task, scales and design as the paper; the images are not the paper's stimuli (38 new Google Street View photos of the same street). Participants answer six questions about themselves, then rate street photos of Jalan Tuanku Abdul Rahman (JTAR), a historic shopping street in Kuala Lumpur lined with outdoor advertisement billboards, one at a time in random order, on the paper's 5-point scale: 1 highly pleasant, 2 pleasant, 3 moderately unpleasant, 4 unpleasant, 5 highly unpleasant. The paper relates the mean rating of each photo to the share of the picture covered by billboards and to its colour (RGB) content, and compares ratings across gender, age, education, living environment and frequency of visits to the city centre.\n\nOriginal study: Adam, Al-Sharaa, Ab Ghafar, Mundher, Abu Bakar & Alhasan (2022), ISPRS International Journal of Geo-Information 11(12), 630. 14 photos from 14 locations along about 700 m of JTAR (Jalan Dang Wangi to Jalan Tun Perak), each shown as the original plus two edited versions with about 5% and 10% more billboard area (42 pictures in random order); 59 respondents with design and architecture backgrounds.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) 38 Google Street View photos (June 2024) from 14 points about 50 m apart along the same stretch, looking along the street and about 40° to each side, instead of the authors' own photos; 4 side views mostly blocked by trees were dropped, as the paper dropped photos blocked by trees or buses; (2) no edited versions with added billboards: billboard area varies only naturally between photos, so a study wanting the paper's controlled +5% / +10% levels must add its own edited images to the folder; (3) photos are taken from the car camera in the road rather than from the sidewalk at eye level; (4) background questions follow the paper's list (gender, age, living environment, education, frequency of visits to the city centre, existing knowledge) with answer options written for this template, and \"existing knowledge\" asked as a design background question. Images: © Google Street View.",
